\f0\fs24 \cf0 You are auditing a Swedish data centre tracker used for academic research.\
\
Input files:\
- data/master/projects_master.csv\
- data/master/capacity_master.csv\
- data/master/sources_master.csv\
- docs/status_rules.md\
- docs/schema.md\
\
Task:\
1. Check each existing project against its registered sources.\
2. Search for newer public sources about each project.\
3. Check whether the current values are still supported:\
   - developer\
   - municipality\
   - bidding_zone\
   - project_status\
   - project-level investment_decision\
   - reported_capacity_mw\
   - interpreted_it_load_mw\
   - capacity_type\
   - capacity_status\
   - capacity-level investment_decision\
   - start_year\
   - construction_start_year\
   - scenario_tag, using docs/status_rules.md rather than preserving old tags by default\
4. When checking scenario_tag, remember that tags are for public display and filtering. They can mark smaller phase rows as relevant to a scenario when those rows sit inside a dated full-site or cumulative project path. Do not use tags alone to decide what is added in scenario totals.\
5. Apply an investment-decision sanity check. Treat investment_decision = yes as a strong claim. It requires public evidence of a committed build, final investment decision, signed customer agreement or lease tied to the capacity, financed construction plan, construction start, or operational facility. Do not use yes only because a project has an announced MW value, expected operation year, permit, site selection, grid discussion, MoU, or marketing page.\
6. Capacity-level investment_decision must be supported for that specific phase or capacity claim. Do not copy commitment from an initial phase to a full-campus potential row. Project-level yes is allowed when at least one material phase is committed, under construction, or operational, even if later phases remain no or unclear.\
7. Cross-check project-level values against capacity-level values using the project-to-capacity consistency rules in docs/status_rules.md.\
8. Do not modify master files.\
9. Write proposed field-level changes to data/audit/suggested_project_updates.csv and data/audit/suggested_capacity_updates.csv.\
10. Write unresolved conflicts to data/audit/source_conflicts.csv.\
11. Write inaccessible URLs to data/audit/dead_links.csv.\
12. Every proposed change must include source_url, source_title, source_date if available, access_date, evidence_excerpt, confidence, and rationale.\
13. If evidence is ambiguous, mark confidence as unclear.\
14. Do not infer capacity from backup generation, reactor capacity, land area, investment value, or number of GPUs unless the source explicitly links it to data centre load or IT capacity.}
